// ============
//
// All executors implement the `Executor` trait. The benchmark runner does not
// know (or care) how a command is actually executed. It only requests a
// measurement for a given command line and receives a `TimingResult` (wall
// clock time, user time, system time and peak memory usage) together with
// the exit status of the command.
//
// Measurement
// -----------
//
// The wall clock time is measured from right before the process is spawned
// until the process has been waited for. User and system time as well as the
// peak memory usage are obtained via `getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN)`, which
// accumulates the resource usage of all terminated (and waited for) child
// processes. The CPU times of a single run are the difference between the
// values before and after the run. The memory usage is the maximum resident
// set size of all child processes so far.
//
// Shell executor
// --------------
//
// By default, commands are executed through a shell (`sh -c <command>`). This
// allows the use of shell features like pipes, redirections or globbing in the
// benchmarked commands. However, spawning the shell itself takes some time.
// In order to correct for this, the shell spawning time is measured before the
// actual benchmarks (by running the shell with an empty command multiple
// times). The mean spawning time is then subtracted from every measurement.
// Since the correction is based on a mean value, individual (corrected)
// measurements could end up being negative for very fast commands. They are
// clamped at zero in this case.
//
// Raw executor
// ------------
//
// With `--shell=none` (or `-N`), commands are split into words (following the
// quoting rules of POSIX shells, but without any expansions) and executed
// directly. No correction is applied in this case. This is useful for very
// fast commands where the noise of the shell spawning time correction would
// be larger than the runtime of the command itself.
//
// Mock executor
// -------------
//
// The mock executor is used in debug mode (`--debug-mode`) only. It does not
// run anything at all. Instead, it expects commands of the form
// `sleep <time>` and reports the given time as the measured wall clock time
// (user and system time as well as the memory usage are reported as zero).
// This allows for fast and fully deterministic test runs of the benchmark
// logic: statistics, the determination of the number of runs, the relative
// speed comparison, and all export formats.
//
// Note that the mock executor also handles all intermediate commands
// (`--setup`, `--prepare`, `--conclude` and `--cleanup`). Their times are
// taken into account in the same way as for the other executors. For
// example, the time of a preparation command counts towards the estimated
// time per benchmark iteration, which determines the number of runs.
//
// If a shell has been specified explicitly, the command line passed to the
// mock executor is the full shell invocation, which is not a valid `sleep`
// command. Commands that do not follow the `sleep <time>` pattern are
// considered programming errors and lead to a panic.
//
// Exit status
